4 LONG W96.94791
3 EXID 4415541
4 TYPE https://gedcom.io/exid-type/FamilySearch-PlaceId
1 FACT Born in France, Department of L'Eure, Canton of Comfort, Parish of Appeville, the 11 of July 1818 [The 24 July 1818 is the date of birth which is listed in his french birth record.] Was raised a farmer boy until sixteen, when he learned a trade, making weaver's reeds. Took a regular course of study in the normal schools of Paris, after which he became a teacher, and was principal of Gospel Society's school at St. Denis, near Paris, where he was in change for twenty-four years. Was secretary of the Protestant Institute of Paris nine years, and was associated with many of leading men of France during the reign of Napoleon 3rd, Was professor four years of phototenic association. For his effective work received three silver medals. He also received a one bronze medal for his efficient work in the deaf and dumb asylum. He left Paris the year before the Franco Prussian war, and came to this country [United States] in 1869. Made his settlement in Seward county in 1873, and took a homestead in "I" precinct. Mr. Leger has a family of twelve children, seven boys and five girls. Mr. Leger is a very intelligent gentleman, being thoroughly versed in all European affairs, and familiar with French history especially. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This biography was written in 1888 by William Wallace Cox and published on page 262 of the "History of Seward County, Nebraska" Lincoln, Nebraska: State Journal Co., 1888. 290 p.
2 TYPE Life sketch
